Details: The bug arises because Fraction.compareTo currently converts fractions to double values and compares the doubles. Due to limited floating-point precision, two different fractions can yield the same double value, causing compareTo to incorrectly return 0 (equal). The failing test case demonstrates this with two fractions approximating PI differently but having identical double values.

Investigation shows Fraction.compareTo implementation:
```java
public int compareTo(Fraction object) {
    double nOd = doubleValue();
    double dOn = object.doubleValue();
    return (nOd < dOn) ? -1 : ((nOd > dOn) ? +1 : 0);
}
```
This approach is imprecise since converting to double loses precision.

The numerator and denominator fields are integers and fractions are stored in lowest terms. The equals method compares fractions by directly comparing numerator and denominator for equality.

To correctly order fractions without precision loss, compareTo should cross multiply numerator and denominator pairs (using long to avoid int overflow) and compare the results instead of converting to double.

This will distinguish fractions that represent nearly equal real numbers but differ in exact value, fixing the compareTo bug and making the test pass.

